 FINAL SOLUTION:
2025-11-27 06:29:35,390 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Short answer
- OpenAI (AI API): single-tenant Bearer API keys (project/admin keys and service accounts) with per-project, per-model rate limits; legacy Completions API exists but the modern, actively developed surface is the Responses API; several older fields are marked deprecated.
- GitHub (code management API): multiple auth methods (fine‑grained PATs, OAuth, and GitHub Apps with short‑lived installation tokens), well-defined per-category rate limits, and extensive, scope/permission-gated endpoints.

Below is a concise, sourced, numbers-backed comparison and a practical plan to verify everything hands‑on.

1) Authentication: how they differ

OpenAI
- Scheme: API key in Authorization: Bearer <key>. Applied globally to https://api.openai.com/v1. Source: OpenAI OpenAPI spec (security: ApiKeyAuth) and API reference home https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference.
- Key management (org/project level):
  - List all admin/org keys: GET /organization/admin_api_keys. Response returns redacted_value, owner, last_used_at, etc. Source: spec endpoint “GET /organization/admin_api_keys” (admin-api-keys-list).
  - List project API keys: GET /organization/projects/{project_id}/api_keys. Each key includes owner (user or service_account), last_used_at. Source: spec endpoint “list-project-api-keys.”
  - Service accounts: List via GET /organization/projects/{project_id}/service_accounts. Source: “list-project-service-accounts.”
- No OAuth scopes; access is governed by which key (admin vs project) you use and the project/org roles associated with the key owner. Source: same endpoints above and API reference.

GitHub
- Auth options and scopes:
  - Personal access tokens (fine-grained or classic) and OAuth app tokens. Scopes constrain access; e.g., GET /user/emails requires scope user:email. Source: https://docs.github.com/rest/overview/authenticating-to-the-rest-api and endpoint docs https://docs.github.com/rest/users/emails#list-email-addresses-for-the-authenticated-user.
  - GitHub Apps (recommended for server-to-server):
    - Authenticate as App using a JWT, then exchange for an installation access token with POST /app/installations/{installation_id}/access_tokens (token TTL 1 hour). Source: endpoint docs https://docs.github.com/rest/apps/apps#create-an-installation-access-token-for-an-app.
    - JWT validity requirements (issued/exp times) and flow: https://docs.github.com/apps/building-github-apps/authenticating-with-github-apps/#authenticating-as-a-github-app.
    - Inspect the authenticated App: GET /app. List installations: GET /app/installations. Sources: https://docs.github.com/rest/apps/apps#get-the-authenticated-app, https://docs.github.com/rest/apps/apps#list-installations-for-the-authenticated-app.

Concrete examples from the specs
- GitHub installation access token:
  - Expires after 1 hour; you can scope to specific repositories and subset of permissions (cannot exceed app’s granted permissions). Source: apps/create-installation-access-token endpoint docs.
- GitHub per-endpoint scopes:
  - GET /user/emails requires user:email; POST/DELETE require user. Source: /user/emails docs.
- OpenAI key visibility and ownership:
  - Project key objects include redacted_value, last_used_at, and owner (user or service account). Source: list-project-api-keys.

2) Rate limiting

GitHub (numbers and categories)
- Check in-band without charging rate limit: GET /rate_limit (calling it “does not count against REST API rate limits”). The response includes separate categories:
  - core, search, code_search, graphql, and others. Example documented limits:
    - core limit 5000 (per hour),
    - search 30,
    - code_search 10.
  - The rate object is deprecated; use resources.core. Source: https://docs.github.com/rest/rate-limit/rate-limit#get-rate-limit-status-for-the-authenticated-user.

OpenAI (per project, per model fields)
- List per-model limits for a project: GET /organization/projects/{project_id}/rate_limits. Fields include:
  - max_requests_per_1_minute,
  - max_tokens_per_1_minute,
  - optional: max_images_per_1_minute, max_audio_megabytes_per_1_minute, max_requests_per_1_day, batch_1_day_max_input_tokens.
- Example (from spec): "ada" with 600 requests/min, 150,000 tokens/min, 10 images/min. Actual numbers vary by project/model; query the endpoint for your project. Source: endpoint “list-project-rate-limits” and its example.

3) Endpoint surface and “what’s modern vs legacy”

OpenAI
- Modern, actively developed surface: POST /responses (Responses API). Supports:
  - streaming, tools/function calling, structured outputs, multi-turn via previous_response_id, and parameters such as temperature (0–2), top_p (0–1), top_logprobs (0–20), service_tier (auto|default|flex|scale|priority). Source: POST /responses in spec; API reference: https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference.
- Chat Completions:
  - GET /chat/completions (list existing) and POST /chat/completions (create). The message.function_call is marked deprecated in favor of tool_calls; system_fingerprint is deprecated. Source: Chat Completions spec excerpt.
- Legacy:
  - POST /completions is labeled “legacy: Completions API.” It has legacy fields like best_of, echo, logprobs, suffix for gpt‑3.5‑turbo‑instruct, etc. Source: /completions spec.
- What to prefer today: Use POST /responses (or Chat Completions as needed). Avoid building new features on legacy Completions. Sources: the OpenAPI spec’s “legacy” labeling and deprecation notes.

GitHub
- Vast surface area of code-management endpoints: 1108 endpoints in v3 REST (source: OpenAPI summary for GitHub).
- Notable deprecations/notes:
  - In /rate_limit response, rate is deprecated; use resources.core.
  - source_import category “no longer in use; will be removed in next API version.” Source: /rate_limit docs.
- Authentication feature maturity:
  - GitHub Apps flow is current best practice for servers (JWT → 1‑hour installation tokens), with granular repo scoping and permission sets. Source: Apps docs and create installation token endpoint.

4) Quantified comparison at a glance

- Base URLs:
  - OpenAI: https://api.openai.com/v1 (219 endpoints in spec v2.3.0). Sources: OpenAI OpenAPI overview; https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference.
  - GitHub: https://api.github.com (1108 endpoints in v3 REST spec v1.1.4). Source: GitHub OpenAPI overview; https://docs.github.com/rest/.
- Auth model:
  - OpenAI: Bearer API keys (admin/org or project keys; service accounts). No scopes; authority comes from key type/owner role. Endpoints exist to list keys and service accounts. Sources: admin-api-keys-list; list-project-api-keys; list-project-service-accounts.
  - GitHub: PAT/OAuth scopes and GitHub Apps JWT→installation tokens (1h TTL) with explicit permissions and optional repository scoping. Sources: /user/emails scopes; Apps auth docs; apps/create-installation-access-token.
- Rate limits:
  - OpenAI: Per-project, per-model limits in requests/min, tokens/min, etc.; discover via GET /organization/projects/{project_id}/rate_limits. Example values in spec: 600 RPM; 150,000 TPM; 10 images/min. Source: list-project-rate-limits.
  - GitHub: Category limits exposed at GET /rate_limit; example documented: core 5000/hour; search 30; code_search 10; request does not count against rate limit. Source: /rate_limit docs.

5) How to “get to the bottom of this” quickly (reproducible steps)

- Confirm your auth works
  - OpenAI:
    - curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $OPENAI_API_KEY" https://api.openai.com/v1/models
    - Then query your actual rate limits: curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $OPENAI_ADMIN_KEY" https://api.openai.com/v1/organization/projects/$PROJECT_ID/rate_limits
    - If you manage keys: curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $OPENAI_ADMIN_KEY" https://api.openai.com/v1/organization/projects/$PROJECT_ID/api_keys
    Sources: /models; list-project-rate-limits; list-project-api-keys.
  - GitHub:
    - PAT/OAuth: curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $GITHUB_TOKEN" https://api.github.com/user (should return your user object).
      Scope needs vary by endpoint; for /user/emails you need user:email. Source: /user; /user/emails.
    - GitHub App flow:
      - Create a JWT (per docs), call GET /app to verify App identity.
      - List installations: GET /app/installations.
      - Exchange to installation token (1h TTL): POST /app/installations/{installation_id}/access_tokens (optionally scope to repos and permissions).
      - Use that token to call repo APIs. Sources: /app, /app/installations, apps/create-installation-access-token, Apps auth guide.

- Validate rate limits live
  - GitHub: curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $GITHUB_TOKEN" https://api.github.com/rate_limit and record resources.core.remaining and reset.
  - OpenAI: Use the project rate limits endpoint above and also observe headers/usage fields on model calls where provided.

- Choose modern endpoints
  - Prefer POST /responses for OpenAI over legacy POST /completions. Confirm deprecated fields (function_call, system_fingerprint) aren’t in your new code paths. Sources: /responses; /completions; Chat Completions spec notes.

- Document decisions with sources
  - Keep links to the exact GitHub and OpenAI docs you used (list below) in your team’s ADR/README.

Authoritative sources (direct, verifiable)
- OpenAI API reference home: https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference
  - Responses API (POST /responses): see API reference; spec excerpt shows temperature (0–2), top_p (0–1), top_logprobs (0–20), service_tier values and conversation fields.
  - Models list (GET /models): https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference/models/list
  - Legacy Completions (POST /completions): marked “legacy” in spec.
  - Project rate limits (GET /organization/projects/{project_id}/rate_limits): per-model max_requests_per_1_minute, max_tokens_per_1_minute, etc.; example 600 RPM, 150,000 TPM, 10 images/min. Docs object: https://platform.openai.com/docs/api-reference/project-rate-limits/object
  - Admin API keys (GET /organization/admin_api_keys) and Project API keys (GET /organization/projects/{project_id}/api_keys): listed with redacted_value, last_used_at, owner.
  - Service accounts (GET /organization/projects/{project_id}/service_accounts).
- GitHub REST API home: https://docs.github.com/rest/
  - Rate limits (GET /rate_limit): “Calling this endpoint does not count against REST API rate limits”; categories include core, search, code_search, graphql; rate object deprecated; example limits: core 5000, search 30, code_search 10. https://docs.github.com/rest/rate-limit/rate-limit#get-rate-limit-status-for-the-authenticated-user
  - Get authenticated user (GET /user): https://docs.github.com/rest/users/users#get-the-authenticated-user
  - Emails (GET /user/emails): requires user:email scope. https://docs.github.com/rest/users/emails#list-email-addresses-for-the-authenticated-user
  - GitHub Apps:
    - Get authenticated App (GET /app): https://docs.github.com/rest/apps/apps#get-the-authenticated-app
    - List installations (GET /app/installations): https://docs.github.com/rest/apps/apps#list-installations-for-the-authenticated-app
    - Create installation access token (POST /app/installations/{installation_id}/access_tokens): 1‑hour token; scoping and permissions. https://docs.github.com/rest/apps/apps#create-an-installation-access-token-for-an-app
    - App auth flow overview (JWT requirements): https://docs.github.com/apps/building-github-apps/authenticating-with-github-apps/#authenticating-as-a-github-app

What this means for your project
- If your immediate need is “call AI models,” OpenAI auth is simpler (static Bearer key) and your main decision is to use the modern Responses API and to budget against your project’s per-model RPM/TPM. Verify your actual quotas via the rate limits endpoint.
- If your need is “operate on GitHub repos/issues/PRs,” use a GitHub App and installation tokens for least-privilege, auditable access. PATs work, but Apps give you granular, revocable permissions and per-installation scoping; confirm rate headroom with /rate_limit and plan for token refresh every hour.
- Outdated paths to avoid:
  - OpenAI: avoid new code on POST /completions; use /responses or Chat Completions. Don’t rely on deprecated message.function_call or system_fingerprint. Sources: spec deprecation notes.
  - GitHub: don’t parse the deprecated rate object; use resources.core. The source_import category is noted as “no longer in use” and will be removed. Source: /rate_limit docs.

If you want, I can provide ready-to-run curl scripts for both stacks (OpenAI Responses create/stream and GitHub App JWT→installation token→repo call) tailored to your environment variables to help you validate all of the above within minutes.
