2025-11-27 06:42:54,686 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Here are the concrete, source-backed numbers you need, with the key conversions already done.

Key assumptions and data
- Heating water from 25°C to 60°C → temperature rise ΔT = 35°C.
- Specific heat of liquid water in this range ≈ 4.18 kJ/kg·°C (4.181 kJ/kg·°C at 25°C; ~4.18–4.19 across 25–60°C). Source: Engineering Toolbox; values consistent with standard thermophysical data.
- Density ~1.0 kg/L; at 25°C it’s 0.997 kg/L (difference <0.3%). Source: Engineering Toolbox.

Basic unit conversions used (authoritative)
- 1 Wh = 3,600 J (definition of joule = W·s). NIST SP 811.
- 1 kWh = 3.6 MJ = 3,412 Btu (EIA).
- 1 Btu (IT) ≈ 1,055.06 J (NIST/EIA).
- 1 m = 3.280839895 ft exactly (because 1 ft = 0.3048 m exactly). NIST.
- 1 US gallon = 3.785411784 L exactly. NIST.

What you’re heating and pipe length
- Temperatures: 25°C = 77°F; 60°C = 140°F.
- Copper pipe length: 5 meters = 16.4042 feet.

Energy to heat the water
Per liter (≈1 kg) from 25°C to 60°C:
- E = m·cp·ΔT ≈ 1 kg × 4.18 kJ/kg·°C × 35°C ≈ 146 kJ per liter.
- In other units:
  - ≈ 145,913 J
  - ≈ 145.913 kJ
  - ≈ 40.531 Wh ≈ 0.04053 kWh
  - ≈ 138.39 Btu
  - ≈ 34.87 kcal
  These values match the converter outputs for 145.913 kJ → Wh/J/Btu/kcal.

Flow rate and resulting power/energy use
Your flow: 0.5 L/min
- As volume/mass flow:
  - 0.5 L/min ≈ 0.1321 US gal/min
  - ≈ 8.333×10^-6 m³/s
  - ≈ 0.5 kg/min (≈0.4985 kg/min if you use 0.997 kg/L at 25°C)
- Thermal energy rate at 0.5 L/min (for ΔT = 35°C):
  - Per minute: ≈ 72.9565 kJ/min ≈ 20.2657 Wh per minute
  - As thermal power: ≈ 1.2159 kW
  - Per hour: ≈ 4,377.39 kJ/h ≈ 1.21594 kWh/h
  - In imperial power: ≈ 4,148.96 Btu/h
  These are direct conversions of the per‑liter figure to your flow rate (all converter-verified).

Quick planning numbers you can use
- Per liter heated 25→60°C: about 0.0405 kWh (≈146 kJ).
- At 0.5 L/min continuously: about 1.22 kW thermal, or 1.22 kWh per hour of operation.
- 10 liters: ≈ 0.405 kWh; 100 liters: ≈ 4.05 kWh of thermal energy.

Notes on accuracy
- Using ρ = 1.000 vs 0.997 kg/L and cp from 4.18–4.19 kJ/kg·°C changes results by <0.5%. The values above are within that engineering tolerance and align with standard references.

Sources (verifiable)
- Specific heat of water (liquid) vs temperature: Engineering Toolbox, “Water - Specific Heat (Cp) vs. Temperature” https://www.engineeringtoolbox.com/specific-heat-capacity-water-d_660.html
- Density of water vs temperature: Engineering Toolbox, “Water - Density vs. Temperature” https://www.engineeringtoolbox.com/water-density-specific-weight-d_595.html
- Energy unit relations:
  - NIST SP 811 (Guide for the Use of the SI): 1 Wh = 3,600 J; exact SI definitions https://www.nist.gov/pml/special-publication-811
  - U.S. EIA Energy conversion: 1 kWh = 3.6 MJ = 3,412 Btu https://www.eia.gov/energyexplained/units-and-calculators/energy-conversion-calculators.php
- BTU to joule: NIST and EIA values (IT Btu ≈ 1,055.06 J) as above.
- Length and gallon conversions (exact): NIST
  - Foot: 1 ft = 0.3048 m exactly → 1 m = 3.280839895 ft https://www.nist.gov/pml/us-surveyfoot
  - US gallon: 1 gal = 3.785411784 L exactly (per NIST/NCWM definitions; see NIST SP 811 link above)

If you’d like, I can also translate the 1.22 kW thermal requirement into an estimated collector area given your local solar insolation and an assumed collector efficiency.
