Tool `Paper Search:search_arxiv` with Parameter {'query': 'Global Carbon Budget 2024 Earth System Science Data', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 5017 tokens): Here’s a concise, information-dense summary of each paper:

1) “Revisiting Generalization Across Difficulty Levels: It's Not So Easy” (Kordi et al.)
- Goal: Assess how LLMs generalize across fine-grained difficulty levels for training/evaluation.
- Method: Rank examples in six datasets via Item Response Theory using outputs from thousands of LLMs; difficulty derived solely from LLM performance, not human labels.
- Findings: Cross-difficulty generalization is limited. Training only on easy or only on hard data fails to consistently improve across the full difficulty spectrum.
- Implication: Curate training/evaluation sets with a range of difficulties; shortcuts by difficulty are risky.

2) “Canvas-to-Image: Compositional Image Generation with Multimodal Controls” (Dalva et al.)
- Problem: Diffusion models struggle with high-fidelity compositional control when combining text, references, spatial layouts, pose, etc.
- Approach: Encode heterogeneous controls into a composite “canvas” image; train with Multi-Task Canvas Training for unified multimodal control understanding.
- Results: Stronger identity preservation and control adherence than SOTA on multi-person composition, pose-controlled composition, layout-constrained generation, and multi-control scenarios.
- Contribution: Unified canvas interface enabling integrated visual-spatial reasoning and generalization to multi-control at inference.

3) “TraceGen: World Modeling in 3D Trace Space Enables Learning from Cross-Embodiment Videos” (Lee et al.)
- Idea: Predict future motion in a compact 3D “trace-space” (scene-level trajectories) instead of pixel space to abstract appearance yet keep geometry for manipulation.
- Data: TraceForge pipeline converts diverse human/robot videos into consistent 3D traces; 123K videos and 1.8M observation-trace-language triplets.
- Performance: After pretraining, with 5 target robot videos achieves 80% success across 4 tasks; with only 5 uncalibrated human handheld phone demos achieves 67.5% success on a real robot.
- Efficiency: 50–600x faster inference than SOTA video-based world models; no object detectors or heavy pixel generation required.

4) “ToolOrchestra: Elevating Intelligence via Efficient Model and Tool Orchestration” (Su et al.)
- Goal: Make tool-augmented reasoning more capable and efficient using small orchestrators.
- Method: Train an 8B Orchestrator with RL using outcome-, efficiency-, and user-preference-aware rewards to coordinate models/tools and align tool choices with user preferences.
- Results: On HLE, 37.1% vs GPT-5’s 35.1% at 2.5x efficiency; on tau2-Bench and FRAMES, surpasses GPT-5 using ~30% of cost. Robust to unseen tools; best performance–cost trade-off across metrics.
- Insight: Lightweight orchestration of diverse tools outperforms heavier agents in both effectiveness and efficiency.

5) “G^2VLM: Geometry Grounded Vision Language Model with Unified 3D Reconstruction and Spatial Reasoning” (Hu et al.)
- Problem: VLMs lack robust spatial intelligence due to missing 3D geometry learning from 2D inputs.
- Approach: Integrate learned 3D visual geometry features into a VLM to directly predict 3D attributes and enhance spatial reasoning via in-context and interleaved reasoning.
- Training: Scalable on multi-view images/videos, leveraging 3D priors typically requiring scarce annotations.
- Results: Comparable to SOTA feed-forward 3D reconstruction; better or competitive on spatial understanding/reasoning tasks. Envisions applications like 3D scene editing; serves as a strong baseline.

6) “Matrix: Peer-to-Peer Multi-Agent Synthetic Data Generation Framework” (Wang et al.)
- Motivation: Synthetic data generation often needs coordinated multi-agent workflows; existing frameworks rely on central orchestrators or are narrow-domain.
- Design: Decentralized, peer-to-peer framework where control/data flow are serialized messages in distributed queues; lightweight agents coordinate; heavy compute via distributed services; built on 


