1) TraceGen: World Modeling in 3D Trace Space Enables Learning from Cross-Embodiment Videos (cs.RO; cs.CV; cs.LG)
- Problem: Learn robot tasks from few demos across embodiments/environments without pixel generation or object detectors.
- Approach: Use a compact, symbolic 3D “trace-space” for scene-level trajectories; TraceGen predicts future motion in trace-space. TraceForge pipeline converts diverse human/robot videos into consistent 3D traces.
- Scale: 123K videos; 1.8M observation–trace–language triplets.
- Results: With 5 target-robot videos: 80% success across 4 tasks; 50–600x faster inference vs SOTA video world models. With 5 uncalibrated human phone demos: 67.5% success on a real robot.
- Significance: Efficient cross-embodiment, cross-environment adaptation using a transferable 3D motion prior.

2) ToolOrchestra: Elevating Intelligence via Efficient Model and Tool Orchestration (cs.CL; cs.AI; cs.LG; cs.MA)
- Problem: Improve performance and efficiency on difficult agentic tasks (e.g., HLE) beyond monolithic LLMs.
- Approach: Train small orchestrators via RL with outcome-, efficiency-, and user-preference-aware rewards to coordinate tools/models.
- Model: Orchestrator (8B).
- Results: HLE 37.1% (vs GPT-5 35.1%) at 2.5x efficiency; on tau2-Bench and FRAMES surpasses GPT-5 while using ~30% cost. Strong performance–cost trade-off; generalizes to unseen tools.
- Significance: Lightweight orchestration of diverse tools can outperform larger models at lower cost.

3) Lesion-Independent Thalamic Degeneration and Cognitive Impairment in MS (q-bio.NC)
- Cohort: 100 MS participants; automatic segmentation of 26 thalamic ROIs; outcome: Symbol Digit Modalities Test (SDMT).
- Findings: 21/26 ROIs associated with SDMT pre-lesion adjustment; 12 remained after adjustment. Lesion-independent associations in global thalamus, sensory relay nuclei (VPL, MGN, LGN), and associative hubs (pulvinar, MD–PF complex).
- Effect sizes: Intrinsically vulnerable nuclei showed lower lesion-mediated effects (13.4%) vs those losing significance post-adjustment (34.2%, p<0.001).
- Implication: IPS impairment reflects both primary and secondary degeneration; nucleus-specific phenotyping may aid risk identification.

4) On Evolution-Based Models for Experimentation Under Interference (stat.ML; cs.LG; cs.SI; econ.EM)
- Problem: Causal inference in networks with unobserved interference pathways.
- Approach: Evolution-based method characterizing how outcome distributions change across rounds/treatments, enabling low-dimensional recursive mappings (distributional DiD analog).
- Theory: Axiomatic conditions for existence/identification; randomization implicitly samples hidden interference channels, enabling learning of heterogeneous spillovers.
- Instances/Extensions: Causal message passing in dense networks; influencer networks; limits under strong temporal trends or endogenous interference.
- Contribution: Identification without explicit network recovery via parallel evolution patterns.

5) DSD: Distributed Speculative Decoding for Edge–Cloud LLM Serving (cs.LG; cs.DC)
- Problem: High latency and limited scalability of LLM decoding in heterogeneous environments; SD mostly single-node.
- Approach: Distributed speculative decoding (coordinated draft–target across devices). DSD-Sim simulator models network/batching/scheduling. Adaptive Window Control dynamically tunes speculation window.
- Results: Up to 1.1x speedup and 9.7% higher throughput vs SD baselines across workloads.
- Significance: More agile, scalable edge–cloud serving.

6) Escaping the Verifier: Learning to Reason via Demonstrations (cs.LG; cs.AI)
