- Focus: Community-based heat adaptation strategies (e.g., interventions affecting neighborhoods/households) and their impact on surface and indoor temperatures and on health outcomes.
- Outcomes assessed: Changes in environmental temperatures (surface/indoor) and health impacts.
- Key note: Synthesizes and evaluates evidence; abstract does not report pooled quantitative effects.

2) Impact of environmental heat on CVD, chronic respiratory diseases, and diabetes in LMICs: systematic review and meta-analysis (Siddiqui et al., 2025; Environmental Research; paper_id 40451411)
- Design: Systematic review and meta-analysis.
- Data/search: Medline, Embase, Web of Science, Global Health, Africa-Wide Information (inception–June 2024); update via PubMed on Apr 29, 2025.
- Yield: 3,884 records screened; 31 studies included; 18 entered meta-analyses.
- Population/setting: Low- and middle-income countries (LMICs).
- Exposure/outcomes: Ambient heat vs. mortality and morbidity for cardiovascular disease (CVD), chronic respiratory disease, and diabetes.
- Main quantitative findings:
  - Per 1°C rise in temperature:
    - CVD mortality risk increased by 2.1%.
    - Respiratory mortality risk increased by 4.1%.
    - CVD morbidity risk increased by 6.7% (higher than mortality effects).
  - Higher heat-related mortality and morbidity among adults ≥65 years and among women.
- Interpretation: Populations in LMICs are vulnerable to heat; elevated morbidity risk may indicate differential access to care and disease management.

