1) 2511.21692v1 — Revisiting Generalization Across Difficulty Levels: It’s Not So Easy
- Question: Do LLMs trained on easy vs. hard data generalize across difficulty bands?
- Method: Rank per-example difficulty in six datasets using outputs from thousands of LLMs and Item Response Theory (IRT), avoiding human difficulty labels.
- Findings: Cross-difficulty generalization is limited; training only on easy or only on hard data fails to yield consistent gains across the full difficulty spectrum.
- Implication: Training and evaluation should include a range of difficulties; shortcutting difficulty is risky.

2) 2511.21691v1 — Canvas-to-Image: Compositional Image Generation with Multimodal Controls
- Problem: Diffusion models struggle with faithful multimodal compositional control (text, references, spatial layout, pose, annotations).
- Approach: Encode heterogeneous controls into a single composite “canvas” image; train with a Multi-Task Canvas Training procedure on curated multi-task datasets.
- Result: Significant gains over SOTA in identity preservation and control adherence across multi-person composition, pose-controlled, layout-constrained, and multi-control generation.
- Benefit: Unified, integrated visual-spatial reasoning that generalizes to multi-control scenarios without task-specific heuristics.

3) 2511.21690v1 — TraceGen: World Modeling in 3D Trace Space Enables Learning from Cross-Embodiment Videos
- Idea: Model future motion in a compact 3D “trace-space” of scene-level trajectories, abstracting away appearance for cross-embodiment learning.
- Data pipeline: TraceForge converts heterogeneous human/robot videos into consistent 3D traces, yielding 123K videos and 1.8M observation–trace–language triplets.
- Results: With 5 target robot videos, 80% success across 4 tasks and 50–600x faster inference than video-based world models; with 5 uncalibrated human phone demos, 67.5% success on a real robot.
- Significance: Efficient adaptation across embodiments without object detectors or heavy pixel-space generation.

4) 2511.21689v1 — ToolOrchestra: Elevating Intelligence via Efficient Model and Tool Orchestration
- Concept: Train small orchestrators (via RL with outcome-, efficiency-, and user-preference-aware rewards) to coordinate tools and models for complex agentic tasks.
- Model: Orchestrator (8B).
- Performance: On HLE, 37.1% vs GPT-5’s 35.1% at 2.5x efficiency; on tau2-Bench and FRAMES, surpasses GPT-5 at ~30% of the cost; robust to unseen tools.
- Takeaway: Lightweight orchestration of diverse tools yields a strong performance–cost trade-off and practical scalability.

5) 2511.21688v1 — G^2VLM: Geometry Grounded Vision Language Model with Unified 3D Reconstruction and Spatial Reasoning
- Goal: Improve VLM spatial intelligence by unifying 3D reconstruction with spatial understanding/reasoning.
- Method: Leverage learned 3D visual geometry features to predict 3D attributes; enhance reasoning via in-context learning and interleaved reasoning; train on multi-view images/videos using 3D priors.
- Results: Comparable to SOTA feed-forward 3D reconstruction and competitive/better performance on spatial understanding and reasoning tasks.
- Impact: Bridges semantic VLM capabilities with low-level 3D vision; scalable and useful for applications like 3D scene editing.

6) 2511.21686v1 — Matrix: Peer-to-Peer Multi-Agent Synthetic Data Generation Framework
- Design: Decentralized, peer-to-peer multi-agent framework where control/data flow as serialized messages through distributed queues; no central orchestrator.
- System: Lightweight agents; heavy operations (LLM inference, containers) as distributed services; built on Ray; scales to tens of thousands of concurrent workflows; modular and configurable.
- Use cases: Multi-agent collaborative dialogue, web-based reasoning data extraction, tool-use trajectory generation in customer service.
- Outcome: 2–15x higher data generation throughput on identical h


