2025-11-27 07:38:36,681 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Here’s a practical, “both/and” way to use the I Ching as a wise lens while grounding your career choice in evidence-based methods. I combined three I Ching readings with research on effective decision-making and career interventions (RCTs and meta-analyses) to give you a clear plan you can trust.

1) What the I Ching is pointing to (and how to use it)
- Hexagram 49 – Revolution: A meaningful change is due, but it succeeds only when the timing and method are right. Don’t force a dramatic leap; align preparation and allies first.
- Hexagram 62 – Small Excess → Hexagram 32 – Duration: Favor small, careful actions that build stability. Sweat the details now to earn long-term consistency and staying power.
- Hexagram 14 – Great Possession (with changing lines): You have (or can access) real assets—skills, experience, relationships, opportunities. Steward them responsibly. Avoid overreach, arrogance, or scattering your energy; focus resources for the good of your team and stakeholders.

Plain English translation:
- Yes, change—but not a reckless jump. Start with precise, low-risk moves that test your direction and cultivate support, then commit to what proves durable.

2) Evidence-backed tools to make a good decision
What the research says (synthesized from randomized trials and meta-analyses):
- Implementation intentions (if-then plans) reliably help people follow through, by linking cues to actions rather than just “motivating harder” (Webb & Sheeran, 2008).
- Mental Contrasting with Implementation Intentions (MCII/WOOP) can improve follow-through in several domains; effectiveness depends on matching plans to real barriers (a large workplace trial found no average benefit when external constraints weren’t addressed).
- Job crafting interventions (small redesigns of tasks, relationships, and learning) can increase engagement and reduce exhaustion; some trials show improved perceptions of employability and performance.
- Decision aids and values clarification: Clarifying what matters and reviewing concrete evidence (especially probabilities/realistic prospects) improves knowledge and reduces decisional conflict; preparation ahead of key conversations helps.
- Retention/fit signals: Supportive colleagues, development opportunities, realistic prospects, and manageable work-family demands strongly influence whether people stay or leave.

Implication for you:
- Pair reflective wisdom with small experiments, if-then plans, and values-based decision criteria. Address real-world constraints (time, money, gatekeepers) upfront.

3) A 90-day roadmap that integrates both
Weeks 1–2: Define “Duration” (your enduring principles)
- List your top 5 values for work (e.g., growth, stability, impact, autonomy, culture, work-life).
- Convert them into weighted decision criteria (e.g., Growth 25%, Stability 20%, Culture 20%, Autonomy 15%, Impact 10%, Work-life 10%).
- WOOP one big wish for the next 90 days (Wish, Outcome, Obstacle, Plan). Make obstacles concrete and external where relevant.

Weeks 2–4: “Small Excess” information sprints
- Schedule 5–7 targeted informational interviews across your options. Ask about:
  - Real day-to-day tasks, support, and development pathways.
  - Culture, manager quality, turnover, workload boundaries.
  - How people grow into the roles you want.
- If possible, shadow for a half-day or do a micro-project to sample the work.

Weeks 3–6: Low-risk experiments
- Run 1–2 “career prototypes” (e.g., scoped side project, short course, certification module, consulting test, internal rotation).
- For each experiment, set if-then plans:
  - If it’s Monday 7–8am, then I complete module 1.
  - If I get stuck on X, then I ask Y by end of day.
- Track feelings (energy, flow) and evidence (performance, feedback).

Weeks 6–8: Job-craft your current role (prove and de-risk)
- Increase tasks that align with your target path (e.g., more client-facing analyses, more product strategy).
- Decrease or delegate misaligned tasks where feasible.
- Seek feedback and micro-wins that bolster your portfolio and references.

Weeks 8–10: Evaluate and prepare the “Revolution”
- Score each option against your weighted criteria using evidence from interviews/experiments.
- Do a pre-mortem: “If this failed in 12 months, what went wrong?” Add mitigations to your plan (runway, skill gaps, allies).
- Line up allies/mentors and confirm timing windows (hiring cycles, budget periods).

Weeks 10–12: Decide and commit (Duration + Great Possession)
- Choose the option that clears your minimum thresholds and has concrete proof from experiments.
- Make a stewardship plan: how you’ll deliver value fast for others (team, customers) and not just yourself.
- Set 3 implementation intentions for the first 30–60–90 days in the new path.

4) Decision scorecard (fast, fair, repeatable)
- Criteria (customize weights): Growth, Stability/Compensation, Culture/Manager, Autonomy, Impact, Work-life Boundaries, Location/Flex.
- For each option, rate 1–5 using evidence you gathered; multiply by weights; sum.
- Tie-breakers: Who are your allies there? What is the clearest next skill ladder? Which option reduces future regrets?

5) Pitfalls to avoid in the next 90 days (echoing Hexagram 14 and 62)
- Big-bang change with thin evidence. Prefer small tests first.
- Overpromising or scattering your assets. Focus resources where they create value for others and for you.
- Ignoring constraints (time, money, gatekeepers). Make plans that fit your real bandwidth.
- Culture blindness. Manager quality and support often trump brand or title.
- Work-family friction creep. If an option demands chronic nights/weekends, discount it unless compensated by outsized growth and clear limits.

6) Prompts to find your “Duration” (enduring principles)
- When in your work did you feel both effective and aligned with who you are?
- Which skills do others consistently seek you out for?
- What kind of problems do you never tire of solving?
- What tradeoffs are you actually willing to make (and which are hard no’s)?

7) How this blends wisdom with evidence
- I Ching themes guide the stance: prepare carefully (Small Excess), time your move (Revolution), commit to what endures (Duration), and steward your assets responsibly (Great Possession).
- Evidence-based tools make it actionable: values clarification and decision aids; WOOP/MCII and if-then plans for follow-through; job crafting and micro-experiments to de-risk; structured scorecards to decide.

If you only do three things this week
- Write your weighted decision criteria and minimum thresholds.
- Book two informational interviews and define one small experiment.
- Draft three if-then plans that protect time and trigger action.

Note
- The I Ching results here use cryptographically secure randomness and are for reflection, not prediction. For major financial/legal decisions, consult qualified professionals.

If you’d like, tell me your two or three concrete options and your top values. I’ll help you build the decision scorecard, the interview questions, and your first set of if-then plans.
