Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'mental contrasting implementation intentions randomized controlled trial', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 3266 tokens): Cross-study summary: Mental Contrasting with Implementation Intentions (MCII) and its WOOP variant (Wish, Outcome, Obstacle, Plan) show promising effects for some health and behavior outcomes (notably snacking/weight and heavy drinking, and in educational/self-regulation contexts), while results are mixed or null in other domains (workplace well-being, sleep).

Key studies

- Online MCII add-on for weight management (Appetite, 2025; NCT05158075; doi:10.1016/j.appet.2025.108209)
  - Design: Online randomized controlled trial with 148 adults with excess weight: MCII (n=50), Sham (n=50), Treatment-as-Usual (TAU; n=48). All received standard care (motivational interviewing, individualized diet and exercise). MCII/Sham: 1-week training; TAU: monitoring.
  - Outcomes: Self-reported snacking (frequency, total servings, ultra-processed food [UPF] servings) and BMI.
  - Results: Significant group × time interactions for snacking frequency [F(2,134)=6.110, p=.003], total servings [F(2,126)=4.291, p=.016], UPF servings [F(2,127)=4.059, p=.020], and BMI [F(2,98)=3.990, p=.022]. MCII outperformed Sham and TAU on all snacking outcomes at post-treatment with large effect sizes; Sham≈TAU (except UPF). BMI reductions greater in MCII in a one-way ANOVA on standardized change [F(2,103)=3.990, p=.006], with moderate effect sizes.
  - Conclusion: MCII improves snacking behavior and BMI beyond standard treatment.

- Workplace health/wellbeing (Int. J. Environ. Res. Public Health, 2025; doi:10.3390/ijerph22030398)
  - Design: Mixed-methods cluster randomized waitlist-controlled trial; 28 UK workplaces; 225 staff. All chose health/wellbeing “wishes.” Intervention: MCII (mental contrasting + implementation intentions); control: deliberation only (waitlist).
  - Outcome: Self-reported progress toward wishes at 4 weeks (1–7 scale).
  - Results: No substantive average effect (mean diff = -0.19; 95% CrI: -1.08 to 0.71). Anxiety increased unexpectedly; signals that men and participants of Asian ethnicity made less progress in the intervention arm. Qualitative themes: insufficient differentiation from other writing/drawing approaches; mismatch between internal motivational focus and external barriers; poor timing of opportunities.
  - Conclusion: No average benefit; implementation/contextual barriers likely limited impact.

- WOOP for heavy drinking in college setting (Health Education & Behavior, 2025; doi:10.1177/10901981241310216)
  - Design: Adults recruited on a college campus randomized to WOOP vs format-matched Sham WOOP (“Wish, Outcome, ‘Outcome,’ Plan”); single-session; delivered by student lay interventionists.
  - Outcome: Heavy drinking days (Alcohol Timeline Follow-Back) at 1- and 2-month follow-ups.
  - Results: WOOP group reported fewer heavy drinking days at both follow-ups vs Sham.
  - Conclusion: Feasible, low-cost, scalable lay-led WOOP reduced heavy drinking in a nonclinical setting.

- WOOP to enhance VA MOVE!/TeleMOVE! (Clinical Trials, 2024; protocol; doi:10.1016/j.cct.2024.107523)
  - Design: Prospective randomized controlled trial protocol for Veterans. Tests WOOP + MOVE!/TeleMOVE! vs MOVE!/TeleMOVE! alone.
  - Rationale/aims: Address motivation barriers to enrollment/engagement; hypothesized to increase program engagement and weight loss.
  - Status: Protocol; outcomes pending.

- MCII for sleep (J Health Psychol, 2023; doi:10.1177/13591053231159168)
  - Design: RCT with 80 university employees (mean age 29.6). MCII + sleep hygiene (SH) vs SH-only. Baseline and post-intervention week assessments.
  - Outcomes: Sleep duration (Fitbit Alta and self-report), sleep quality, daily stress; cortisol awakening response (CAR).
  - Results: Overall increases in self-reported sleep quality and duration across participants, but no meaningful condition × week interactions for sleep parameters or CAR. Higher average stress linked to shorter duration and lower quality; within-person higher stress days preceded lower-quality nights.
