 FINAL SOLUTION:
2025-11-27 07:48:30,770 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Here’s a concise, evidence-backed synthesis of what matters about BRAF mutations in melanoma, how they influence treatment selection, what’s FDA‑approved now, and what the strongest trials show (with citations you can use in a presentation).

1) What BRAF mutations mean in melanoma
- Frequency and types: About 40–50% of cutaneous melanomas harbor BRAF mutations. Most are V600E (~70–80% of BRAF) and V600K (~10–20%). Non‑V600 (e.g., K601E, L597) are uncommon and generally don’t benefit from approved BRAF inhibitors to the same degree.
- Biology and resistance: BRAF V600 activates MAPK signaling. Single‑agent BRAF inhibition quickly led to resistance via MAPK reactivation (e.g., BRAF amplification/splice variants, NRAS/MEK mutations) or PI3K–AKT signaling. Combining a BRAF inhibitor with a MEK inhibitor both improves efficacy and reduces paradoxical MAPK activation in wild‑type cells (which decreases secondary cutaneous SCCs).

2) Targeted therapy options (metastatic/unresectable, BRAF V600E/K)
All three BRAF/MEK pairs are FDA‑approved and deliver high response rates (typically 60–70%), fast tumor shrinkage, and median PFS ~11–15 months. Key datasets:
- Dabrafenib + trametinib (Tafinlar + Mekinist)
  - COMBI‑d / COMBI‑v: improved PFS and OS vs monotherapy; updated 5‑year OS ~34% overall, higher in favorable‑risk cohorts; toxicity notable for pyrexia with dabrafenib.
  - Citations: Robert C NEJM 2015; Long GV Lancet Oncol 2016; Robert C Lancet Oncol 2019 (5‑yr outcomes).
- Vemurafenib + cobimetinib (Zelboraf + Cotellic)
  - coBRIM: median PFS 12.3 vs 7.2 months vs vemurafenib, improved OS; photosensitivity and rash more with vemurafenib.
  - Citation: Larkin J NEJM 2014; Ascierto PA Lancet Oncol 2016/2019 updates.
- Encorafenib + binimetinib (Braftovi + Mektovi)
  - COLUMBUS: median PFS 14.9 months vs 7.3 with vemurafenib; OS ~33.6 months vs 16.9. Generally favorable tolerability (less pyrexia).
  - Citation: Dummer R Lancet Oncol 2018; Dummer R JCO 2020/Ann Oncol 2021 updates.

Triplet (PD‑L1 + BRAF/MEK):
- Atezolizumab + cobimetinib + vemurafenib received FDA approval (2020) after IMspire150 showed PFS benefit (15.1 vs 10.6 months; HR 0.78). OS benefit was not clearly demonstrated; toxicity higher than BRAF/MEK alone. Use is selective.
- Citation: Gutzmer R Lancet 2020 (IMspire150).

3) Immunotherapy options (apply regardless of BRAF status)
These are foundational in BRAF‑mutant disease because of durability of benefit:
- Anti‑PD‑1 monotherapy: pembrolizumab or nivolumab.
- Nivolumab + ipilimumab (CTLA‑4): higher response and deepest durability at the cost of more toxicity.
  - CheckMate 067: long‑term OS benefit; 5+ year OS ~52% with nivo+ipi vs ~44% with nivolumab vs ~26% with ipilimumab alone.
  - Citations: Larkin J NEJM 2015; Hodi FS NEJM 2018; Larkin J JCO 2022 (6.5‑yr).
- Nivolumab + relatlimab (anti‑LAG‑3): improved PFS vs nivolumab in first‑line metastatic melanoma with a more favorable AE profile than nivo+ipi.
  - RELATIVITY‑047: PFS HR ~0.75–0.80; benefit consistent in BRAF‑mutant subgroup.
  - Citations: Tawbi HA NEJM 2022; JCO 2023 update.

4) Sequencing: Immunotherapy first or BRAF/MEK first?
Two prospective trials addressed this in BRAF V600 metastatic melanoma:
- DREAMseq (EA6134): Starting with nivolumab + ipilimumab followed by dabrafenib + trametinib at progression achieved superior 2‑year OS (72% vs 52%) vs the reverse sequence. Conclusion: immunotherapy‑first improves survival when feasible.
  - Citation: Atkins MB JCO 2023 (EA6134/DREAMseq).
- SECOMBIT: Three‑arm strategy (IO→TT, TT→IO, or brief TT “sandwich” then IO). Four‑year data support IO‑first; “sandwich” strategy also performed well.
  - Citation: Ascierto PA Lancet Oncol 2023.

Practical takeaways:
- If clinically stable: start with immunotherapy (nivo+ipi or nivo+relatlimab; PD‑1 mono when combination toxicity is a concern).
- If rapid disease control is needed (high tumor burden, symptomatic, organ compromise): BRAF/MEK targeted therapy upfront is reasonable for rapid responses, with a plan to transition to immunotherapy if/when possible.

5) Brain metastases
- BRAF/MEK: Dabrafenib + trametinib shows intracranial ORR ~58% in V600E, but responses tend to be less durable than with IO.
  - Citation: Davies MA Lancet Oncol 2017 (COMBI‑MB).
- Immunotherapy: Nivolumab + ipilimumab produces high and durable intracranial responses in asymptomatic, steroid‑naïve patients; outcomes are worse when symptomatic/on steroids.
  - Citations: Tawbi HA Lancet Oncol 2018; Long‑term update Neuro Oncol 2021. ABC trial (Menzies AM Lancet Oncol 2018).

6) Adjuvant and neoadjuvant settings (resected disease)
- Targeted therapy (BRAF V600E/K, stage III): Dabrafenib + trametinib improves relapse‑free survival with durable benefit; recent long‑term follow‑up shows sustained OS advantage.
  - Citation: Long GV NEJM 2017 (COMBI‑AD); 8‑year update 2023 (Lancet/NEJM reports).
- Anti‑PD‑1 adjuvant:
  - Stage III: nivolumab (CheckMate 238) and pembrolizumab (KEYNOTE‑054) reduce recurrence vs ipilimumab/placebo.
  - Stage IIB–IIC: pembrolizumab (KEYNOTE‑716) and nivolumab (CheckMate 76K) approved based on improved RFS/DMFS.
  - Citations: Weber J NEJM 2017 (CheckMate 238); Eggermont AM NEJM 2018 (KEYNOTE‑054); Luke JJ NEJM 2022 (KEYNOTE‑716); CheckMate 76K 2023 (press/JCO).
- Neoadjuvant immunotherapy: Not yet an FDA‑labeled indication, but multiple trials (OpACIN‑neo/PRADO; SWOG S1801) support neoadjuvant or perioperative IO to deepen pathologic responses and improve event‑free survival. NCCN endorses neoadjuvant IO in select stage III patients.
  - Citations: Rozeman EA Nat Med 2019; Menzies AM Lancet Oncol 2022; Patel SP NEJM 2023 (SWOG S1801).

7) 2023–2025 notable FDA approvals and updates relevant to BRAF‑mutant melanoma
- Lifileucel (Amtagvi), autologous TIL therapy: FDA approved (Feb 2024) for unresectable/metastatic melanoma after progression on PD‑1–based therapy and, if BRAF‑mutant, after BRAF/MEK therapy. ORR ~31% with durable responses in heavily pretreated patients.
  - Citations: FDA news release/label 2024; Sarnaik AA Lancet Oncol 2021; Iovance C‑144‑01 pooled update JCO 2023.
- Atezolizumab + cobimetinib + vemurafenib: FDA approval (2020) remains an option for first‑line BRAF V600‑mutant disease; use varies due to modest incremental PFS and added toxicity vs BRAF/MEK alone.
  - Citation: Gutzmer R Lancet 2020 (IMspire150).
- Anti‑PD‑1 adjuvant expansions (stage IIB–IIC): Pembrolizumab (2021–22) and nivolumab (2022–23) approvals; check current labels for exact staging language.
  - Citations: KEYNOTE‑716 NEJM 2022; CheckMate 76K 2023.

8) Toxicities and practical selection
- BRAF/MEK class: pyrexia (dabrafenib>encorafenib), rash/photosensitivity (vemurafenib), arthralgia, fatigue; MEK‑related cardiomyopathy, ocular/retinal events, CPK elevation. Secondary cutaneous neoplasms are less frequent with BRAF/MEK combos than with BRAF monotherapy.
- Immunotherapy: immune‑related adverse events (dermatologic, endocrine, hepatic, GI). Nivo+ipi has highest efficacy and toxicity; nivo+relatlimab and PD‑1 monotherapy have lower toxicity.

9) Testing and eligibility notes
- All patients with unresectable/metastatic melanoma should have timely testing for BRAF mutations (IHC or PCR/NGS). Distinguish V600E vs V600K (V600K can respond slightly less to BRAF/MEK but still benefits). Non‑V600 BRAF mutations do not have approved targeted therapies; consider MEK inhibitor off‑label/clinical trial depending on mutation and context.

10) Where clinical trials are heading
- Optimizing sequence (IO→TT vs TT→IO): data favor IO‑first for most.
- “Triplets” (IO + BRAF/MEK): atezo+cobi+vem is approved; other triplets (e.g., PD‑1 + dab/tram or PD‑1 + encor/bin) have shown mixed efficacy and higher toxicity without clear OS advantage; not standard.
  - Citations: KEYNOTE‑022 (pembro + dab/tram; Lancet Oncol 2020 update) and COMBI‑i (spartalizumab + dab/tram; Lancet Oncol 2020) did not meet primary OS/PFS endpoints.
- Neoadjuvant/perioperative IO: moving toward standard in select stage III.
- Post‑PD‑1 options: TIL therapy (lifileucel) and clinical trials are central.

Key references (landmark and recent)
- BRAF/MEK in metastatic disease:
  - Robert C et al. N Engl J Med. 2015;372:30–39. COMBI‑v (dabrafenib+trametinib vs vemurafenib).
  - Long GV et al. Lancet Oncol. 2016;17:1248–1260. COMBI‑d.
  - Robert C et al. Lancet Oncol. 2019;20:1239–1251. Five‑year outcomes dabrafenib+trametinib.
  - Larkin J et al. N Engl J Med. 2014;371:1867–1876. coBRIM (vemurafenib+cobimetinib).
  - Dummer R et al. Lancet Oncol. 2018;19:603–615. COLUMBUS (encorafenib+binimetinib). Updates: JCO 2020; Ann Oncol 2021.
- Triplet therapy:
  - Gutzmer R et al. Lancet. 2020;395:1835–1844. IMspire150 (atezolizumab+cobimetinib+vemurafenib).
- Immunotherapy (metastatic):
  - Larkin J et al. N Engl J Med. 2015;373:23–34. CheckMate 067 initial.
  - Larkin J et al. J Clin Oncol. 2022;40:127–137. 6.5‑year CheckMate 067 update.
  - Tawbi HA et al. N Engl J Med. 2022;386:24–34. RELATIVITY‑047 (nivo+relatlimab).
- Sequencing:
  - Atkins MB et al. J Clin Oncol. 2023;41:1865–1875. DREAMseq (EA6134).
  - Ascierto PA et al. Lancet Oncol. 2023;24:177–189. SECOMBIT (4‑yr).
- Brain metastases:
  - Davies MA et al. Lancet Oncol. 2017;18:863–873. COMBI‑MB (dabrafenib+trametinib).
  - Tawbi HA et al. Lancet Oncol. 2018;19:672–681. CheckMate 204 (nivo+ipi intracranial).
- Adjuvant:
  - Long GV et al. N Engl J Med. 2017;377:1813–1823. COMBI‑AD (dabrafenib+trametinib). Long‑term update 2023.
  - Weber J et al. N Engl J Med. 2017;377:1824–1835. CheckMate 238 (nivolumab vs ipilimumab).
  - Eggermont AM et al. N Engl J Med. 2018;378:1789–1801. KEYNOTE‑054 (pembrolizumab vs placebo).
  - Luke JJ et al. N Engl J Med. 2022;387:121–132. KEYNOTE‑716 (stage IIB–IIC pembrolizumab).
  - CheckMate 76K: 2023 reports (nivolumab in stage IIB–IIC).
- TIL therapy:
  - FDA approval: Feb 2024 (Amtagvi/lifileucel) — FDA press and label.
  - Sarnaik AA et al. Lancet Oncol. 2021;22:1345–1356. C‑144‑01 results. JCO 2023 pooled analyses.

How to present this succinctly
- Lead with the clinical algorithm: “Test for BRAF; if V600E/K and patient is stable, start immunotherapy (nivo+ipi or nivo+relatlimab). If rapid control is needed, start BRAF/MEK. In brain metastases, prioritize nivo+ipi if asymptomatic; use BRAF/MEK when quick intracranial control is needed. In resected stage III BRAF V600, offer adjuvant dabrafenib+trametinib or anti‑PD‑1; in stage IIB–IIC, use adjuvant anti‑PD‑1. After PD‑1±CTLA‑4 and BRAF/MEK, consider TIL therapy (lifileucel).”
- Anchor key statements with the trials above (DREAMseq/SECOMBIT for sequencing; COMBI‑AD and KEYNOTE‑716/CheckMate 76K for adjuvant; CheckMate 204/COMBI‑MB for brain; IMspire150 for the triplet approval).

If you want, I can tailor a slide‑ready one‑pager with the exact hazard ratios, medians, and footnoted citations for each decision point.
